A||||6||CW85T-Type||Stupid Clutch!||||1||
Z||000000||CW85T-Type||09-22-2001||06:11 PM||||I've been having a problem with the clutch in my 85.  I put in a Izusu 5spd in and it was fine for a while but now I can't get the clutch to disengage fully.  There is no air in the Hyd system and I checked the bracket doesn't look bent (nice straight angle).  I checked and there is only 3/4 of an inch of movement of the rod at the slave.<P>I have replaced the master with a used one (dont have the $100 for a replacement) and its the same.  (3/4") I replaced the slave with a new cast iron one and same thing.<P>There is no air in the system.<P>What else could it be?  I'm about ready to go back to my old steel slave that may or may not be good....<P><P>------------------<BR>Chris Wilson<BR>1985 Regal T-Type 12.7@114<BR>1985 Fiero SE (V6, 5spd Izusu, WS6)||||reg||1
Z||000001||Phil||09-22-2001||06:57 PM||||Get a small magnet and try sticking it to your clutch pedal - if the magnet doesn't stick, you have an aluminium clutch pedal and it IS BENT. Replace it with a steel one and all should be fine again.||||reg||1
Z||000002||jelly2m8||09-22-2001||07:43 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Phil:<BR><B>Get a small magnet and try sticking it to your clutch pedal - if the magnet doesn't stick, you have an aluminium clutch pedal and it IS BENT. Replace it with a steel one and all should be fine again.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Another quick tell tale on the bent clutch pedal is to look in at both the clutch and the brake pedal. The clutch pedal should look like it is 3/4 - 1 " out further than the brake pedal. Any less, oddas are the pedal is bent. <P> BTW I have seen 3 or 4 bent STEEL clutch pedals. Just because it may be the steel one, doesnt mean it isn/t bent. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||William Federle||09-22-2001||09:34 PM||||Before I replaced my clutch master cyl two yrs ago, I bled the system so that no more bubbles were produced, but I still didn't have enough travel to disengage, and if the<BR>pedal was held down, the slave slowly retracted. Someone at work said that this could occur if the seal in the master cyl was defective letting the pressure that should be going to the slave leak by the seal in the M/S. So I replaced the M/S and got past that problem. Maybe a rebuild kit would fix your master cyl.<P>I also replaced the alum. clutch with a steel one from the fiero store.<P><BR>Good luck.<p>[This message has been edited by William Federle (edited 09-22-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||frankshel||09-22-2001||10:17 PM||||As an machinist in earlier years, I fixed the problem in one shot. Don't go wasting your money on a lot of parts. Here is the foolproof way to fix it...get a round or square piece of steel about 3"  long. Measure the shaft size on the clutch plunger. Drill a hole in one end somewhere near the center on a drill press. A lathe would be ideal for round stock but not necessary to work. Hold it up against the plunger that is hooked to the clutch pedal and get an idea on how much material would have to be left if you drilled the same size hole in the other end if you cut the rod in half and then removed approximately 1 inch in between. Since you have the old rod, don't worry about ruining it. Cut the inch out. Then drill a hole through the other side of the block and unhook the pedal, slide the block in between and see if you have the free play you need so the clutch will release. Repeat as necessary until you have the free play you need to let the clutch disengage.A rule of thumb is that if you remove an inch and need free play, you need to leave 1/2" between th drilled holes to give you the extra 1/2" you need so the clutch will release. The strength of the piece of steel with a good fit will keep the alignment. Mine has been in for years and I have fixed many others for people. If you need adjustment in time, drop a brass beebe from a beebe gun in one end, it will take care of any adjustment you will need.If you are satisfied, fill both holes with epoxy, slide the rod ends in and they will never move or wear out. Any question, give me a call. Tonight (Saturday night) I am at my summer house..518-585-7357  work # days...518-273-4110 x 310...Frank Sheldon  Nights or weekends, 518-383-6585. I have 5 of them and can give you a lot of advice. I still have questions from time to time so I do use this website..I am only human!! Good luck,<BR>Frank Sheldon<BR>Ticonderoga, NY||||reg||10
Z||000005||frankshel||09-22-2001||10:24 PM||||PS...From Frankshel...I misread your message.....To make it DISENGAGE...just do the opposite....cut the rod , remove a piece, drill a 3" piece of rod of steel and when you drill the holes, knowing that you removed 1/2", leave 3/4" to 1" in the block to give you a little extra push to disengage the clutch. I have done about 8 like this too and have not had any problems... The throw out bearing won't stay engaged, there is plenty of free play for that...trust me....I have never had a problem with doing this, but I sure cured a lot of frustration!! AND SAVED ME A LOT OF MONEY BESIDES.....Give me a call if necessary..Frank Sheldon  (Saturday night)   518-585-7357.<BR>Good luck||||reg||1
Z||000006||CW85T-Type||09-26-2001||06:56 PM||||Its fixed!<P>It was the clutch pedal.<P>I went to Fiero Motorsports and picked one up along with a new pushrod for the master cylinder.  It now shifts smoooooth... <P>Thanks to everyone!<P>||||reg||10
